      "filename": "Historic Ireland - Glasnevin Cemetery Is a Hidden Gem And Well Worth a Visit (5544809301).jpg",
      "title": "File:Historic Ireland - Glasnevin Cemetery Is a Hidden Gem And Well Worth a Visit (5544809301).jpg",
      "url": "https://upload.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/6/60/Historic_Ireland_-_Glasnevin_Cemetery_Is_a_Hidden_Gem_And_Well_Worth_a_Visit_%285544809301%29.jpg",
      "descriptionUrl": "https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Historic_Ireland_-_Glasnevin_Cemetery_Is_a_Hidden_Gem_And_Well_Worth_a_Visit_(5544809301).jpg",
      "width": 4592,
      "height": 3056,
      "mime": "image/jpeg",
      "license": "CC BY-SA 2.0",
      "licenseUrl": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/2.0",
      "artist": "William Murphy from Dublin, Ireland",
      "credit": "Historic Ireland - Glasnevin Cemetery Is a Hidden Gem And Well Worth a Visit",
      "source": "Historic Ireland - Glasnevin Cemetery Is a Hidden Gem And Well Worth a Visit (5544809301)",
      "description": "The Glasnevin Museum Was Officially Opened  Thursday, 8 April, 2010 \n\nExtract from Speech by the Taoiseach, Mr. Brian Cowen:\n\"The Museum celebrates almost 180 years of Glasnevin - 180 years of Irish history - of the social, historical, political and artistic development of modern Ireland. It contains  over 1.5 million archived burial records covering everyone buried here, including most of Ireland's revolutionary leaders, such as Eamon DeValera, Michael Collins, Constance Markievicz, Cathal Brugha - other towering figures in Irish history like Daniel O'Connell, Charles Stewart Parnell and Arthur Griffith -  - famous  poets and writers like Gerard Manley Hopkins, Brendan Behan and Kate Cruise O'Brien - great sportsmen like Michael Cusack and Liam Whelan - soldiers from every war since the 1830s - victims of the Famine - the Angels - and all those who lived ordinary lives, many of them extraordinary people in their own way, and who found their place of rest in Glasnevin - 1.5 million lives who once were part of the life of this country and whose memory we honour in this hallowed place.\nThe detailed burial records, which are preserved both in the original handwritten journals and online, will enable visitors to quickly locate the resting places of ancestors and loved ones. They also constitute a  fantastic resource for researchers.\n\nThe genealogical services provided by Glasnevin Museum are greatly enhanced by the Milestone Gallery. This houses the interactive digital \"Timeline\", containing details of the lives of hundreds of interesting people buried here; and it will host a series of special exhibitions on key historical figures. The Museum also contains the fascinating City of the Dead exhibition dedicated to Glasnevin itself, as well as conference rooms and a lecture hall. Very fittingly, the first exhibition to be held here will celebrate the life and achievements of Glasnevin's founder - that giant of Irish history, Daniel O'Connell.\"",
      "dateCreated": "Taken on 19 March 2011, 13:51",
